<TeXmacs|2.1.4>

<style|<tuple|notes|notes-meta>>

<\body>
  <notes-header>

  <chapter*|Proofs and complements: beyond the real line>

  <with|font-shape|italic|Observables <math|\<cdot\>> Companion II. November 10, 2026.>

  Companion to <notes-link|Observables beyond the real line|self-adjoint-operators-extensions.tm>.

  <with|font-series|bold|Earlier proof companions.> <notes-link|I. The basic idea|self-adjoint-operators-proofs.tm>.

  <\notes-abstract>
  This companion follows Part II through strongly commuting families and the
  construction of their joint spectral measure, then normal operators,
  manifold-valued spectral measures, other outcome spaces, normal states and
  geometric commutators. The final two sections justify the post's further
  perspectives on distance and quantization. It uses the spectral calculus proved in the Part I
  companion. The normal spectral theorem uses a short closed-graph argument
  given below for its positive square-root reduction. Mackey's full
  classification and the exploratory geometric questions are cited
  background, not further theorems proved here.
  </notes-abstract>

  Each operator includes its domain. Inner products are conjugate-linear in
  the first argument and linear in the second. Section and theorem numbers
  are local to this companion; references to another companion name its part
  explicitly.

  <with|font-series|bold|Contents.>

  <\enumerate>
    <item> <hlink|Strongly commuting families and their joint spectral
    measure|#sec:joint>

    <item> <hlink|Normal operators and the complex plane|#sec:normal>

    <item> <hlink|Manifold-valued observables and other outcome
    spaces|#sec:manifolds>

    <item> <hlink|Observables, normal states and probability
    laws|#sec:probability>

    <item> <hlink|The geometric commutator and the distance
    formula|#sec:geometry>

    <item> <hlink|Sharp observables and Poisson brackets|#sec:quantization>
  </enumerate>

  <section|Strongly commuting families and their joint spectral
  measure><label|sec:joint>

  <subsection*|Commutation of full observables><label|sec:commutation>

  The diagonal example at the start of the post is the atomic case of the
  theorem below. There the coordinate-axis projections satisfy
  <math|P<rsub|1>P<rsub|2>=0> and <math|P<rsub|1>+P<rsub|2>=I>.
  Assigning them to <math|<around*|(|1,2|)>> and
  <math|<around*|(|-1,3|)>> defines a normalized PVM with two atoms.
  Its coordinate integrals are the displayed matrices, and multiplying
  finite spectral sums gives the displayed joint calculus. Its marginals
  are obtained by inverse image under the coordinate maps. The theorem
  below proves the same assertions without an atomic assumption.

  Let <math|A,B> be self-adjoint. We first justify the equivalences used in
  the post. Strong commutation means <math|E<rsub|A><around*|(|\<Delta\>|)>*E<rsub|B><around*|(|\<Gamma\>|)>=E<rsub|B><around*|(|\<Gamma\>|)>*E<rsub|A><around*|(|\<Delta\>|)>>
  for all Borel sets. Simple spectral integrals and bounded convergence show
  that this implies commutation of <math|f<around*|(|A|)>> and <math|g<around*|(|B|)>> for every
  bounded Borel <math|f,g>. In particular all nonreal resolvents, and all
  members of the two unitary groups, commute.

  Conversely suppose all nonreal resolvents commute. The algebra generated by
  <math|<around*|(|x-i|)><rsup|-1>> and <math|<around*|(|x+i|)><rsup|-1>> is uniformly dense in
  <math|C<rsub|0><around*|(|\<bbb-R\>|)>>, by Stone\UWeierstrass. Thus the two continuous
  calculi commute. Adjoin constants and fix a bounded operator commuting with
  one calculus. The bounded Borel functions whose spectral integrals commute
  with it are closed under uniformly bounded pointwise limits, because such
  spectral integrals converge strongly. The functional monotone-class theorem
  therefore gives all bounded Borel functions. Applying this argument
  successively to the two calculi gives commuting spectral projections.

  For the unitary criterion, use a bounded operator <math|T> commuting with
  <math|e<rsup|i*t*A>> for every real <math|t>. For <math|h,k\<in\>H>, the
  finite complex measures <math|<around*|\<langle\>|h,T*E<rsub|A><around*|(|\<cdummy\>|)>*k|\<rangle\>>>
  and <math|<around*|\<langle\>|h,E<rsub|A><around*|(|\<cdummy\>|)>*T*k|\<rangle\>>> have equal
  Fourier transforms. Uniqueness of the Fourier transform of finite measures
  makes them equal, so <math|T> commutes with every spectral projection of
  <math|A>. First take <math|T=e<rsup|i*s*B>>, then repeat the argument for
  <math|B> with <math|T=E<rsub|A><around*|(|\<Delta\>|)>>. This proves strong commutation
  from the unitary-group criterion.

  These conditions imply the weak form identity on the full intersection
  <math|D<around*|(|A|)>\<cap\>D<around*|(|B|)>>. For example, the joint cutoffs in the proof below
  approximate each vector together with its <math|A>- and <math|B>-images;
  the identity holds on each bounded joint spectral subspace and passes to
  the limit. In contrast, the periodic/antiperiodic momentum example
  satisfies both algebraic and form commutation on
  <math|C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|0,1|)>> without strongly commuting. The graph
  norm of either momentum is the <math|H<rsup|1>> norm. The closure of that
  test space is <math|H<rsub|0><rsup|1><around*|(|0,1|)>>, whose functions vanish at both
  endpoints, a proper subspace of either self-adjoint boundary domain. Thus
  the test space is not an operator core.

  For completeness, let <math|S<rsub|\<theta\>>=-i*d/d*x> on
  <math|D<around*|(|S<rsub|\<theta\>>|)>=<around*|{|u\<in\>H<rsup|1><around*|(|0,1|)>:u<around*|(|1|)>=e<rsup|i*\<theta\>>u<around*|(|0|)>|}>>.
  Integration by parts shows that a vector in the adjoint domain has a weak
  derivative in <math|L<rsup|2>>, hence lies in <math|H<rsup|1>>; the
  boundary term vanishes for every <math|u\<in\>D<around*|(|S<rsub|\<theta\>>|)>> exactly
  when the same boundary condition holds for the adjoint vector. Thus
  <math|S<rsub|\<theta\>>> is self-adjoint. Multiplication by
  <math|e<rsup|i*\<theta\>*x>> carries the Fourier basis
  <math|e<rsup|2*\<pi\>*i*k*x>> to a complete eigenbasis, which verifies the
  stated periodic and antiperiodic spectral projections directly.

  The failure of this particular test space to be a core is not a general
  converse criterion. Algebraic commutation on a common invariant core need
  not imply the bounded commutation identities proved above; Nelson's
  <hlink|Analytic vectors|https://doi.org/10.2307/1970331> contains the
  classical counterexample. That construction is cited as a further warning;
  the criteria proved here are commutation of resolvents, spectral
  projections or full unitary groups.

  <subsection*|Constructing the measure from the coordinate projections>

  <\theorem>
    [Joint spectral measure of a finite family]<label|thm:joint> Let
    <math|A<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,A<rsub|d>> be pairwise strongly commuting
    self-adjoint operators, with spectral measures <math|E<rsub|j>>. There is
    a unique normalized PVM <math|E> on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>> such that

    <\equation*>
      E<around*|(|\<Delta\><rsub|1>\<times\>\<cdots\>\<times\>\<Delta\><rsub|d>|)>
      =E<rsub|1><around*|(|\<Delta\><rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\>*E<rsub|d><around*|(|\<Delta\><rsub|d>|)>
    </equation*>

    for all Borel sets <math|\<Delta\><rsub|j>>. It gives a nondegenerate
    representation <math|J:C<rsub|0><around*|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>|)>\<rightarrow\>B<around*|(|H|)>>
    satisfying

    <\equation*>
      J<around*|(|f<rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>f<rsub|d>|)>
      =f<rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\>*f<rsub|d><around*|(|A<rsub|d>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The coordinate integrals are exactly the given operators, including
    domains. Every real-valued Borel function <math|h>, finite at every point, defines a
    self-adjoint joint spectral integral with domain
    <math|<around*|{|u:<big|int><around*|\||h|\|><rsup|2>*d*\<mu\><rsub|u>\<less\>\<infty\>|}>>,
    where <math|\<mu\><rsub|u><around*|(|F|)>=<around*|\<langle\>|u,E<around*|(|F|)>*u|\<rangle\>>>. Conversely,
    the coordinate operators of a normalized PVM on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>
    are pairwise strongly commuting.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    For a rectangle <math|R=\<Delta\><rsub|1>\<times\>\<cdots\>\<times\>\<Delta\><rsub|d>>,
    put <math|P<around*|(|R|)>=<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|d>E<rsub|j><around*|(|\<Delta\><rsub|j>|)>>.
    Commutation makes this product self-adjoint and idempotent, hence an
    orthogonal projection. Its range is the intersection of the coordinate
    ranges: the product fixes every vector in that intersection, and each
    factor fixes the product's range. Also <math|P<around*|(|R|)>*P<around*|(|S|)>=P<around*|(|R\<cap\>S|)>>. In
    particular disjoint rectangles give orthogonal projections.

    Partition each real coordinate into finitely many disjoint Borel sets,
    including the complement of any bounded region being considered. The
    resulting grid partitions <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>> into finitely many
    rectangles <math|R<rsub|\<alpha\>>>. Their projections are orthogonal and
    sum to <math|I>, by distributivity and normalization of the
    <math|E<rsub|j>>. For a function constant on these cells define

    <\equation*>
      J<rsub|0><around*|(|s|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>c<rsub|\<alpha\>>*P<around*|(|R<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>,
      <space|1em>s=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>c<rsub|\<alpha\>>*1<rsub|R<rsub|\<alpha\>>>.
    </equation*>

    Refining a coordinate partition subdivides its spectral projection into
    the corresponding finite sum. Two grids have a common refinement, so this
    definition is independent of the representation of <math|s>. Refinement
    also proves linearity, multiplication and preservation of complex
    conjugation. Orthogonality gives, for every <math|u\<in\>H>,

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<\|\|\>|J<rsub|0><around*|(|s|)>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>
      =<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>><around*|\||c<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|><rsup|2>*<around*|\<\|\|\>|P<around*|(|R<rsub|\<alpha\>>|)>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>
      \<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|s|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>><rsup|2>*<around*|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    Compactly supported continuous functions can be approximated uniformly by
    such step functions: enclose the support in a larger box, divide the box
    into sufficiently small half-open rectangular cells, sample the function
    on each cell, and put zero outside. Uniform continuity controls the
    error, including the cells near the support's boundary. Continuous
    functions vanishing at infinity are then approximated by first cutting
    them off. The norm estimate defines a unique contractive map
    <math|J:C<rsub|0><around*|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>|)>\<rightarrow\>B<around*|(|H|)>>. Taking uniform
    limits in the identities for <math|J<rsub|0>> shows that <math|J> is a
    <math|\<ast\>>-homomorphism. Applying the same approximation to each
    one-variable factor proves the tensor-product formula in the statement.

    Choose continuous compactly supported functions
    <math|0\<leqslant\>\<chi\><rsub|n>\<leqslant\>1> on <math|\<bbb-R\>>
    tending pointwise to one. The one-dimensional calculus gives
    <math|\<chi\><rsub|n><around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)>\<rightarrow\>I> strongly. Their finite
    products also tend strongly to <math|I>: expand the difference of the
    products as a telescoping sum and use that every factor has norm at most
    one. Thus <math|J> is nondegenerate.

    Apply the representation\UPVM theorem of <notes-link|the Part I
    companion|self-adjoint-operators-proofs.tm#thm:pvm> to the locally
    compact, second-countable space <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>. Specifically,
    Riesz\UMarkov represents each positive functional
    <math|f\<mapsto\><around*|\<langle\>|u,J<around*|(|f|)>*u|\<rangle\>>> by a finite Radon measure.
    Polarization produces its operator-valued measure; multiplicativity,
    extended from continuous functions by bounded monotone approximation,
    makes its values projections. Nondegeneracy supplies total projection
    <math|I>. This gives a PVM <math|E> with <math|J<around*|(|f|)>=<big|int>f*d*E>. No
    joint measure was used in constructing <math|J>.

    To identify its marginals, fix <math|j> and
    <math|h\<in\>C<rsub|0><around*|(|\<bbb-R\>|)>>. Apply the tensor formula to
    <math|h<around*|(|x<rsub|j>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|k\<neq\>j>\<chi\><rsub|n><around*|(|x<rsub|k>|)>>.
    The operator side tends strongly to <math|h<around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)>>. Bounded
    convergence for the PVM integral makes the other side tend strongly to
    <math|<big|int>h<around*|(|x<rsub|j>|)>*d*E<around*|(|x|)>>. Equality for all such <math|h>,
    scalar measure uniqueness and polarization identify the marginal with
    <math|E<rsub|j>>. A rectangle is the intersection of its coordinate
    cylinders; multiplication of the corresponding spectral projections now
    gives exactly the asserted rectangle formula.

    For uniqueness, two candidate PVMs give finite scalar measures
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|u,E<around*|(|\<cdummy\>|)>*u|\<rangle\>>> agreeing on every Borel
    rectangle. Rectangles form a generating pi-system containing the whole
    space. The pi-lambda theorem makes these scalar measures equal on all
    Borel sets, and polarization makes the PVMs equal. The marginal identity
    recovers each <math|A<rsub|j>> with its original spectral domain.
    Integrating a real Borel <math|h> that is finite at every point uses the real spectral-integral
    theorem of Part I, or its proof with cutoffs
    <math|1<rsub|<around*|\||h|\|>\<leqslant\>n>>; it gives exactly the stated domain and
    self-adjointness. Finally, starting with any joint PVM, its coordinate
    projections are the projections of cylinders. They commute because all
    values of a PVM do. This proves the converse.
  </proof>

  The examples in the post follow directly from uniqueness. Coordinate
  multiplication on <math|L<rsup|2><around*|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>,d*x|)>> has
  <math|E<around*|(|F|)>*u=1<rsub|F>*u>, because this PVM has the required rectangle
  projections. Its scalar law is <math|<around*|\||u|\|><rsup|2>*d*x>. For the pair
  <math|<around*|(|A,A|)>>, push <math|E<rsub|A>> forward by <math|x\<mapsto\><around*|(|x,x|)>>; on
  a rectangle it gives <math|E<rsub|A><around*|(|\<Delta\>\<cap\>\<Gamma\>|)>>, which is
  the required product. Thus the joint law is concentrated on the diagonal,
  and compatibility alone does not imply independence. Likewise the
  pushforward under a Borel map <math|F> is a PVM since inverse images
  preserve intersections, complements and countable disjoint unions; the
  spectral substitution rule follows first for indicators, then bounded
  simple functions, and finally by spectral truncation.

  The common coordinate domain is described by
  <math|<big|int><around*|\||x|\|><rsup|2>*d*\<mu\><rsub|u><around*|(|x|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>, because
  <math|<around*|\||x|\|><rsup|2>=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|d>x<rsub|j><rsup|2>>. The
  subspaces <math|E<around*|(|<around*|[|-n,n|]><rsup|d>|)>*H> lie in every coordinate domain and
  have dense union. For a vector in the common domain, these cutoffs converge
  together with every coordinate image, by dominated convergence. This
  justifies the joint-cutoff argument in the commutation discussion.

  <subsection*|Addition in the joint calculus><label|sec:strong-sums>

  <\theorem>
    [Strongly commuting sums]<label|thm:strong-sums> If all spectral projections of
    <math|A=A<rsup|\<ast\>>> commute with all those of
    <math|B=B<rsup|\<ast\>>>, the algebraic sum on
    <math|D<around|(|A|)>\<cap\>D<around|(|B|)>> is essentially self-adjoint.
    Its closure is the joint spectral integral of <math|x+y>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:joint> constructs the joint PVM <math|E> from
    the spectral projections of <math|A,B>. Integrating the real function
    <math|x+y> against it gives a self-adjoint operator <math|C>. On
    <math|D<around|(|A|)>\<cap\>D<around|(|B|)>>, joint truncation and the
    inequality <math|<around|\||x+y|\|><rsup|2>\<le\>2*x<rsup|2>+2*y<rsup|2>>
    prove <math|C*u=A*u+B*u>.

    For <math|u\<in\>D<around|(|C|)>>, let
    <math|u<rsub|n>=E<around|(|<around|[|-n,n|]><rsup|2>|)>*u>. Then
    <math|u<rsub|n>\<in\>D<around|(|A|)>\<cap\>D<around|(|B|)>>, and both
    <math|u<rsub|n>\<to\>u> and <math|C*u<rsub|n>\<to\>C*u> by dominated
    convergence with <math|1+<around|\||x+y|\|><rsup|2>>. The restricted sum
    is therefore a core restriction of <math|C>. This proves the claim and
    also explains why the closure may have a larger domain than the original
    intersection. For any bounded Borel <math|f>, uniqueness of the spectral
    calculus gives <math|f<around*|(|C|)>=<big|int>f<around*|(|x+y|)>*d*E<around*|(|x,y|)>>. Taking
    <math|f<around*|(|s|)>=e<rsup|i*t*s>> and multiplying bounded joint spectral
    integrals gives <math|e<rsup|i*t*C>=e<rsup|i*t*A>*e<rsup|i*t*B>>. For
    <math|B=-A>, the spectral sum is the everywhere-defined zero operator;
    this verifies the example <math|B=-A> in the post.
  </proof>

  <subsection*|The unitary representation of the additive group>

  For the PVM just constructed define

  <\equation*>
    U<around*|(|t|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*t\<cdot\>x>*d*E<around*|(|x|)>
    =<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|d>e<rsup|i*t<rsub|j>*A<rsub|j>>.
  </equation*>

  The equality follows by multiplication in the bounded Borel calculus. That
  multiplication also gives <math|U<around*|(|s+t|)>=U<around*|(|s|)>*U<around*|(|t|)>> and
  <math|U<around*|(|t|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=U<around*|(|-t|)>>. For every vector <math|u>,

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|(|U<around*|(|t|)>-U<around*|(|s|)>|)>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>
    =<big|int><around*|\||e<rsup|i*t\<cdot\>x>-e<rsup|i*s\<cdot\>x>|\|><rsup|2>*d*\<mu\><rsub|u><around*|(|x|)>
    \<rightarrow\>0<space|1em><around*|(|t\<rightarrow\>s|)>
  </equation*>

  by dominated convergence, with dominating constant four. Thus this is a
  strongly continuous unitary representation of <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>.
  Conversely, let <math|U> be such a representation. Its restrictions to the
  coordinate axes are strongly continuous unitary groups, so Stone's theorem
  gives self-adjoint generators <math|A<rsub|j>>. These groups commute by the
  group law. The equivalence proved above shows that their generators are
  pairwise strongly commuting. Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:joint> constructs
  their joint PVM, whose representation agrees with <math|U> because every
  group element is a sum of coordinate-axis elements. Uniqueness follows from
  Stone's theorem and uniqueness of the joint PVM.

  One can also recover the measure directly from the scalar Fourier tests
  <math|<around*|\<langle\>|u,U<around*|(|t|)>*u|\<rangle\>>>. For completeness, if a finite complex
  measure <math|\<nu\>> on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>> has zero Fourier
  transform, convolve it with a centered Gaussian density
  <math|g<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> of covariance <math|\<varepsilon\>*I>. The
  Gaussian Fourier inversion formula and Fubini apply since the Gaussian
  Fourier transform is integrable and <math|\<nu\>> has finite total
  variation. They give <math|g<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*\<ast\>*\<nu\>=0>. Hence
  for <math|f\<in\>C<rsub|c><around*|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>|)>>,
  <math|<big|int><around*|(|f*\<ast\>*g<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>*d*\<nu\>=0>. The Gaussian
  approximate identity converges uniformly on such <math|f>, so
  <math|<big|int>f*d*\<nu\>=0>. Riesz\UMarkov uniqueness gives
  <math|\<nu\>=0>. Apply this to the difference of two scalar spectral
  measures and polarize. This proves the Fourier characterization used in the
  post without presupposing its coordinate operators.

  <section|Normal operators and the complex plane><label|sec:normal>

  <\lemma>
    [Closed-graph square-root lemma] If <math|S> is closed and densely
    defined, then <math|T=S<rsup|\<ast\>>*S> is positive self-adjoint,
    <math|D<around*|(|T<rsup|1/2>|)>=D<around*|(|S|)>>, and <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|T<rsup|1/2>u|\<\|\|\>>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|S
    u|\<\|\|\>>> on this domain.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The graph of <math|S> is closed in <math|H\<oplus\>H>, and its orthogonal
    complement consists of <math|<around*|(|-S<rsup|\<ast\>>v,v|)>> with
    <math|v\<in\>D<around*|(|S<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>>. Decompose <math|<around*|(|y,0|)>> along these two
    closed subspaces. The second component gives <math|v=-S u>; hence
    <math|u\<in\>D<around*|(|S<rsup|\<ast\>>S|)>> and <math|<around*|(|I+S<rsup|\<ast\>>S|)>u=y>.
    Thus <math|I+T> is onto. It is injective and its inverse has norm at most
    one, since <math|<around*|\<langle\>|u,<around*|(|I+T|)>u|\<rangle\>>\<geqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>.
    That everywhere-defined inverse is symmetric, hence self-adjoint; its
    injectivity and dense range imply that <math|T> is self-adjoint.

    Set <math|Q<rsub|n>=n<around*|(|n+T|)><rsup|-1>>. The spectral theorem gives
    <math|Q<rsub|n>\<rightarrow\>I> strongly and
    <math|Q<rsub|n>H\<subseteq\>D<around*|(|T|)>>. Applying the preceding argument to
    <math|S<rsup|\<ast\>>> also makes <math|S*S<rsup|\<ast\>>> positive
    self-adjoint. For <math|u\<in\>D<around*|(|S|)>> the resolvent equation, applied to
    <math|w=<around*|(|n+T|)><rsup|-1>u>, gives <math|S
    Q<rsub|n>u=n<around*|(|n+S*S<rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsup|-1>S u>. The right side tends to
    <math|S u> by the spectral theorem. On <math|D<around*|(|T|)>> the identity is
    <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|S v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around*|\<langle\>|v,T
    v|\<rangle\>>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|T<rsup|1/2>v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>. Therefore
    <math|T<rsup|1/2>Q<rsub|n>u> is Cauchy when <math|u\<in\>D<around*|(|S|)>>;
    closedness of <math|T<rsup|1/2>> gives <math|u\<in\>D<around*|(|T<rsup|1/2>|)>> and
    the asserted norm identity. Conversely, if <math|u\<in\>D<around*|(|T<rsup|1/2>|)>>,
    the same bounded functions of <math|T> converge in the graph norm of
    <math|T<rsup|1/2>>. The norm identity on <math|D<around*|(|T|)>> makes <math|S
    Q<rsub|n>u> Cauchy, and closedness of <math|S> gives <math|u\<in\>D<around*|(|S|)>>.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    [The normal spectral theorem and bounded transform]<label|thm:normal> Let
    <math|N> be densely defined and closed. The following conditions are
    equivalent: <math|N<rsup|\<ast\>>*N=N*N<rsup|\<ast\>>>, including
    domains; and <math|D<around|(|N|)>=D<around|(|N<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>> with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|N*u|\<\|\|\>>=<around|\<\|\|\>|N<rsup|\<ast\>>*u|\<\|\|\>>>
    there. Under these conditions there is a unique PVM <math|E> on
    <math|\<bbb-C\>>, with <math|E<around|(|\<bbb-C\>|)>=I>, such that

    <\equation*>
      D<around|(|N|)>=<around*|{|u:<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-C\>><around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*<around|\<langle\>|u,E<around|(|z|)>*u|\<rangle\>>\<less\>\<infty\>|}>,<space|2em>N=<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-C\>>z*<space|0.17em>d*E<around|(|z|)>.
    </equation*>

    Conversely every such PVM defines a normal operator. These objects also
    correspond to nondegenerate representations of
    <math|C<rsub|0><around|(|\<bbb-C\>|)>> and to bounded normal contractions
    <math|T> with <math|ker <around|(|I-T<rsup|\<ast\>>*T|)>=<around|{|0|}>>.
    The last correspondence is

    <\equation*>
      T=N*<around|(|I+N<rsup|\<ast\>>*N|)><rsup|-1/2>.
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      D<around|(|N|)>=<math-up|Ran><around|(|I-T<rsup|\<ast\>>*T|)><rsup|1/2>.
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      N=T*<around|(|I-T<rsup|\<ast\>>*T|)><rsup|-1/2>.
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    The preceding closed-graph lemma applied to <math|N> gives
    <math|P=<around*|(|N<rsup|\<ast\>>N|)><rsup|1/2>> with <math|D<around*|(|P|)>=D<around*|(|N|)>> and
    <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|P u|\<\|\|\>>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|N u|\<\|\|\>>>
    there. Applied to <math|N<rsup|\<ast\>>>, it shows that the two
    conditions in the theorem are equivalent: equal positive operators have
    the same square-root domains and norms; conversely equality of the two
    domains and squared norms gives equality of their closed quadratic forms
    by polarization. An operator <math|S<rsup|\<ast\>>S> is recovered from
    that form: <math|u\<in\>D<around*|(|S<rsup|\<ast\>>S|)>> exactly when
    <math|v\<mapsto\><around*|\<langle\>|S u,S v|\<rangle\>>> on <math|D<around*|(|S|)>> is
    represented by a vector of <math|H>, namely <math|S<rsup|\<ast\>>S u>.
    Thus the positive operators agree.

    Define <math|V*<around|(|P*u|)>=N*u> on <math|<math-up|Ran>P>. This is
    well-defined and isometric by the norm identity. It extends to an
    isometry from <math|<around|(|ker N|)><rsup|\<perp\>>> onto
    <math|<wide|<math-up|Ran>N|\<bar\>>=<around|(|ker
    N<rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsup|\<perp\>>>. Normality gives <math|ker N=ker
    N<rsup|\<ast\>>>, so extending this isometry by the identity on <math|ker
    N> yields a unitary <math|V> on <math|H>. Thus <math|N=V*P> on
    <math|D<around|(|P|)>>, and <math|N<rsup|\<ast\>>=P*V<rsup|\<ast\>>> with
    its natural domain. In particular <math|N*N<rsup|\<ast\>>=V*P<rsup|2>*V<rsup|\<ast\>>>.
    Normality says <math|V*P<rsup|2>*V<rsup|\<ast\>>=P<rsup|2>>. The
    uniqueness of the nonnegative self-adjoint square root gives
    <math|V*P*V<rsup|\<ast\>>=P>. It also follows that <math|V> commutes with
    the bounded calculus of <math|P>: it commutes with its resolvents, hence
    with the continuous calculus and, by bounded pointwise limits, with its
    spectral projections.

    Put <math|Q=<around|(|I+P<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1/2>> and <math|T=V*P*Q>. The
    positive calculus of <math|P> shows that <math|P*Q> is bounded by one,
    that <math|Q> is injective, and that <math|<math-up|Ran>Q=D<around|(|P|)>>.
    For the last equality, <math|Q*v\<in\>D<around|(|P|)>> because
    <math|t<rsup|2>/<around|(|1+t<rsup|2>|)>\<leq\>1>; conversely
    <math|u\<in\>D<around|(|P|)>> has <math|v=<around|(|I+P<rsup|2>|)><rsup|1/2>*u\<in\>H>
    and <math|Q*v=u>. Commutation with <math|V> gives

    <\equation*>
      T<rsup|\<ast\>>*T=T*T<rsup|\<ast\>>=P<rsup|2>*<around|(|I+P<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>I-T<rsup|\<ast\>>*T=Q<rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    Consequently <math|T> is a normal contraction and <math|ker
    <around|(|I-T<rsup|\<ast\>>*T|)>=<around|{|0|}>>.

    Let <math|F> be its bounded spectral measure on the closed unit disc. The
    spectral projection <math|F<around|(|<around|{|<around|\||w|\|>=1|}>|)>>
    is the kernel projection of <math|I-T<rsup|\<ast\>>*T>, hence is zero.
    The map <math|g<around|(|w|)>=w/<sqrt|1-<around|\||w|\|><rsup|2>>> is a
    homeomorphism from the open disc to <math|\<bbb-C\>>. Define
    <math|E<around|(|B|)>=F<around|(|g<rsup|-1><around|(|B|)>|)>>; the
    discarded boundary has zero projection, so
    <math|E<around|(|\<bbb-C\>|)>=I>. Its scalar quadratic measures are
    finite Radon measures, as all finite Borel measures on these
    second-countable locally compact spaces are Radon.

    We record the domain argument for complex spectral integrals. Given any
    PVM on <math|\<bbb-C\>>, let <math|E<rsub|R>=E<around|(|<around|{|<around|\||z|\|>\<leq\>R|}>|)>>
    and <math|N<rsub|R>=<big|int>z*1<rsub|<around|{|<around|\||z|\|>\<leq\>R|}>>*<space|0.17em>d*E>.
    Orthogonality and the bounded calculus give
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|N<rsub|R>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<big|int><rsub|<around|\||z|\|>\<leq\>R><around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|u>>.
    Thus <math|N<rsub|R>*u> converges exactly on the asserted domain, which
    is dense since <math|E<rsub|R>\<to\>I> strongly. Its limit defines
    <math|N> there. If <math|u<rsub|n>\<to\>u> and <math|N*u<rsub|n>\<to\>v>,
    then <math|N<rsub|R>*u=E<rsub|R>*v> for every <math|R>. Hence
    <math|<big|int><rsub|<around|\||z|\|>\<leq\>R><around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|u>\<leq\><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>;
    monotone convergence gives <math|u\<in\>D<around|(|N|)>> and
    <math|N*u=v>. This proves closedness.

    Let <math|<wide|N|\<bar\>>> denote the integral of
    <math|<wide|z|\<bar\>>> in this paragraph only. Bounded truncation and
    convergence give <math|<around|\<langle\>|N*u,v|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|u,<wide|N|\<bar\>>*v|\<rangle\>>>
    on the common domain, so <math|<wide|N|\<bar\>>\<subset\>N<rsup|\<ast\>>>.
    If <math|v\<in\>D<around|(|N<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>> and
    <math|N<rsup|\<ast\>>*v=w>, testing against <math|E<rsub|R>*u> gives
    <math|<around*|(|<big|int><wide|z|\<bar\>>*1<rsub|<around|\||z|\|>\<leq\>R>*<space|0.17em>d*E|)>*v=E<rsub|R>*w>.
    The same norm estimate forces <math|v\<in\>D<around|(|<wide|N|\<bar\>>|)>>,
    proving equality. The scalar measure of <math|N*u> is
    <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>*\<mu\><rsub|u>> (test first on bounded
    sets and pass to the limit). It follows that

    <\equation*>
      D*<around|(|N<rsup|\<ast\>>*N|)>=D*<around|(|N*N<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>=<around*|{|u:<big|int><around|\||z|\|><rsup|4>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|u>\<less\>\<infty\>|}>,<space|2em>N<rsup|\<ast\>>*N=N*N<rsup|\<ast\>>=<big|int><around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*E.
    </equation*>

    The fourth-moment condition implies the second-moment condition since
    <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>\<leq\>1+<around|\||z|\|><rsup|4>>. Thus
    every complex spectral integral is normal.

    Apply this construction to the pushforward of <math|F>. For its resulting
    operator <math|N<rsub|0>=g<around|(|T|)>>, write
    <math|S=<around|(|I-T<rsup|\<ast\>>*T|)><rsup|1/2>>. The domain condition
    for <math|g<around|(|T|)>> is equivalent to integrability of
    <math|<around|(|1-<around|\||w|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>, because
    <math|<around|\||g<around|(|w|)>|\|><rsup|2>=<around|(|1-<around|\||w|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>-1>.
    The bounded spectral calculus therefore gives
    <math|D<around|(|N<rsub|0>|)>=<math-up|Ran>S> and <math|N<rsub|0>*S=T>:
    one direction follows by applying <math|S> to any vector, and the other
    by applying the spectral integral of <math|<around|(|1-<around|\||w|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1/2>>
    to a vector in <math|D<around|(|N<rsub|0>|)>>. In the construction from
    <math|N>, <math|S=Q> and <math|N*Q=T>, so the domains and actions of
    <math|N<rsub|0>> and <math|N> coincide.

    Conversely the same argument starts from any normal contraction with the
    stated kernel condition and constructs <math|N>. The identity
    <math|<around|(|I+<around|\||g<around|(|w|)>|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1/2>=<sqrt|1-<around|\||w|\|><rsup|2>>>
    proves that its bounded transform is precisely <math|T>. If another PVM
    represented the same <math|N>, the already proved product formula would
    make its integral of <math|z/<sqrt|1+<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>>> the same
    <math|T>. Uniqueness of the bounded normal spectral measure and
    injectivity of this scalar map force the two PVMs to agree. Finally the
    representation\UPVM theorem in <notes-link|the Part I
    companion|self-adjoint-operators-proofs.tm#thm:pvm>, whose proof applies
    to any second-countable locally compact space, identifies these measures
    with the nondegenerate representations of
    <math|C<rsub|0><around|(|\<bbb-C\>|)>>.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    [Real and imaginary parts and two-parameter
    evolution]<label|prop:normal-pair> For the preceding <math|N>, the
    spectral integrals <math|A=<big|int><math-up|Re>z*<space|0.17em>d*E> and
    <math|B=<big|int><math-up|Im>z*<space|0.17em>d*E> are strongly commuting
    self-adjoint operators, <math|D<around|(|N|)>=D<around|(|A|)>\<cap\>D<around|(|B|)>>,
    and <math|N=A+i*B> on that domain. Moreover <math|D<around|(|N|)>> is a
    core for both <math|A> and <math|B>; consequently the closures of
    <math|<around|(|N+N<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>/2> and
    <math|<around|(|N-N<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>/<around|(|2*i|)>> are <math|A> and
    <math|B>. Conversely every strongly commuting self-adjoint pair arises
    this way. The corresponding strongly continuous unitary representation is

    <\equation*>
      U<around|(|s,t|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*<around|(|s<math-up|Re>z+t<math-up|Im>z|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*E<around|(|z|)>=e<rsup|i*s*A>*e<rsup|i*t*B>.
    </equation*>

    Every strongly continuous unitary representation of
    <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>> is obtained uniquely in this manner.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The real spectral-integral theorem makes <math|A,B> self-adjoint. Their
    projections are <math|E<around|(|<around|{|z:<math-up|Re>z\<in\>C|}>|)>>
    and <math|E<around|(|<around|{|z:<math-up|Im>z\<in\>D|}>|)>>, so they
    commute. The domain identity follows from
    <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>=<around|(|<math-up|Re>z|)><rsup|2>+<around|(|<math-up|Im>z|)><rsup|2>>;
    truncation then gives the asserted sum. If <math|u\<in\>D<around|(|A|)>>,
    put <math|u<rsub|R>=E<rsub|R>*u>. These vectors belong to
    <math|D<around|(|N|)>>, converge to <math|u>, and
    <math|A*u<rsub|R>\<to\>A*u> by dominated convergence of the squared real
    coordinate. This proves the core assertion for <math|A>; the proof for
    <math|B> uses the imaginary coordinate.

    Conversely, Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:joint> constructs the joint PVM
    of any strongly commuting self-adjoint pair <math|A,B>. Push this measure
    forward by <math|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<mapsto\>x+i*y> and apply
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:normal>. Its coordinate integrals and their
    domains agree with <math|A,B>; the identity
    <math|<around*|\||x+i*y|\|><rsup|2>=x<rsup|2>+y<rsup|2>> proves that the normal
    integral is <math|A+i*B> on precisely their domain intersection.

    The assertion about unitary representations is the case <math|d=2> of the
    result proved in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:joint>. Under the same
    identification of the plane, its characters are
    <math|e<rsup|i*<around*|(|s*\<Re\>z+t*\<Im\>z|)>>>. Thus the joint Fourier integral
    gives the displayed formula, and the converse and uniqueness follow from
    the coordinate-axis Stone generators, as proved there.
  </proof>

  On <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<bbb-C\>,d<rsup|2>*z|)>> multiplication by
  <math|z> has the PVM of multiplication by set indicators, so it is normal.
  For any <math|z<rsub|0>> and <math|r\<gtr\>0>, the normalized indicator of
  the disc <math|<around|\||z-z<rsub|0>|\|>\<less\>r> belongs to its domain
  and satisfies <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|N-z<rsub|0>|)>*u<rsub|r>|\<\|\|\>>\<leq\>r>.
  A bounded inverse would imply <math|1\<leq\><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|N-z<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>*r>,
  impossible as <math|r*\<downarrow\>*0>. Hence its spectrum is all of
  <math|\<bbb-C\>>. For <math|t\<ne\>0> the multiplier <math|e<rsup|i*t*z>>
  has modulus <math|e<rsup|-t<math-up|Im>z>> and is unbounded; unit vectors
  supported in discs tending in the appropriate imaginary direction show this
  directly. These examples justify the two limitations on resolvents and
  one-parameter exponentials stated in Section 2 of the post.

  <section|Manifold-valued observables and other outcome
  spaces><label|sec:manifolds>

  <\proposition>
    [Changing the outcome space]<label|prop:manifold-pvm> Let <math|M> be a
    second-countable Hausdorff manifold. Normalized PVMs on <math|M>
    correspond bijectively to nondegenerate representations of
    <math|C<rsub|0><around|(|M|)>>. For such a PVM, every real-valued Borel
    function <math|h> defines a self-adjoint spectral integral with domain
    <math|<around|{|u:<big|int><around|\||h|\|><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|u>\<less\>\<infty\>|}>>.
    A Borel map <math|F:M\<to\>L>, where <math|L> is another such manifold,
    gives a PVM <math|E<rsub|F><around|(|B|)>=E<around|(|F<rsup|-1><around|(|B|)>|)>>
    and the substitution rule <math|g<around|(|E<rsub|F>|)>=<around|(|g\<circ\>F|)><around|(|E|)>>,
    including equality of the maximal domains for unbounded real Borel
    <math|g>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    A second-countable manifold is locally compact and admits an increasing
    sequence of compactly supported continuous cutoffs tending to one.
    Continuous functions separate points and generate its Borel
    sigma-algebra. These are precisely the ingredients used in the
    representation\UPVM proof in <notes-link|the Part I
    companion|self-adjoint-operators-proofs.tm#thm:pvm>: Riesz\UMarkov
    produces finite regular scalar measures, polarization produces the
    operator measures, multiplication follows by the functional
    monotone-class theorem, and nondegeneracy gives total mass <math|I>. This
    proves the first assertion with the same construction and uniqueness,
    without a coordinate choice.

    Inverse images preserve complements and disjoint countable unions, so
    <math|E<rsub|F>> is a normalized PVM. The substitution formula holds for
    indicator functions, then simple functions, and then bounded Borel
    functions by uniform simple approximation. For unbounded real <math|g>,
    the scalar pushforward identity gives
    <math|<big|int><around|\||g|\|><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsup|F><rsub|u>=<big|int><around|\||g\<circ\>F|\|><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|u>>,
    so the domains coincide. Apply the bounded identity to
    <math|g*1<rsub|<around|\||g|\|>\<leq\>n>> and pass to the defining
    spectral-integral limit. Taking <math|L=\<bbb-R\>> and <math|F=h> gives
    the self-adjoint assertion.

    If <math|F> is continuous, <math|g\<circ\>F> is bounded continuous for
    <math|g\<in\>C<rsub|0><around|(|L|)>>, but need not vanish at infinity.
    Its integral is already available in the bounded Borel extension. For
    example the constant map from a noncompact <math|M> pulls a function
    nonzero at its image point back to a nonzero constant. If <math|F> is
    proper, the sets <math|<around|{|<around|\||g\<circ\>F|\|>\<geq\>\<varepsilon\>|}>>
    are compact, proving that the pullback then lies in
    <math|C<rsub|0><around|(|M|)>>. Thus no properness hypothesis is silently
    needed for the substitution rule.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    [Coordinates on the circle, sphere and an embedded
    manifold]<label|prop:manifold-coordinates> A PVM on <math|S<rsup|1>> is
    equivalently a unitary operator. A PVM on <math|S<rsup|2>> is
    equivalently a commuting triple of bounded self-adjoint operators
    <math|X<rsub|1>,X<rsub|2>,X<rsub|3>> with
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>X<rsub|a><rsup|2>=I>. Given a proper embedding
    <math|\<iota\>:M\<to\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|k>>, a PVM on <math|M> is
    equivalently a strongly commuting self-adjoint <math|k>-tuple whose joint
    PVM is concentrated on <math|\<iota\><around|(|M|)>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    On the circle, integration of <math|z> gives a unitary since
    <math|<wide|z|\<bar\>>*z=1>; the bounded spectral theorem recovers its
    PVM, and density of polynomials in <math|z,<wide|z|\<bar\>>> gives
    uniqueness. On the sphere the three coordinate integrals commute, are
    bounded and self-adjoint, and satisfy the stated relation. Conversely, a
    commuting triple of bounded self-adjoint operators is strongly commuting.
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:joint> gives its joint PVM <math|E>. Its
    coordinate marginals are supported in bounded intervals, so <math|E> is
    concentrated in a bounded box. Let <math|p<around*|(|x|)>=x<rsub|1><rsup|2>+x<rsub|2><rsup|2>+x<rsub|3><rsup|2>-1>.
    The assumed relation and multiplication in the bounded calculus on that
    box give <math|p<around*|(|E|)>=0>. Thus <math|<big|int><around*|\||p<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|2>*d*\<mu\><rsub|u><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|p<around*|(|E|)>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=0>
    for every <math|u>. Positivity gives zero scalar mass to
    <math|<around*|\||p|\|>\<geqslant\>1/n> for every positive integer <math|n>, hence to
    the complement of the sphere. Polarization gives the same assertion for
    <math|E>. Restrict this measure to <math|S<rsup|2>>. Its coordinate
    integrals are exactly the given operators. Coordinate polynomials
    separate points on <math|S<rsup|2>> and are closed under conjugation, so
    Stone\UWeierstrass and scalar measure uniqueness prove uniqueness.

    For the last assertion, push the PVM forward by <math|\<iota\>> and
    integrate the real coordinate functions. Their spectral projections
    commute because they all belong to the same PVM. Conversely a finite
    strongly commuting tuple has the joint PVM constructed in
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:joint>. The embedding is a homeomorphism onto
    its image, which is closed by properness. A joint PVM concentrated there
    can therefore be transported back by <math|\<iota\><rsup|-1>>. This
    reverses both constructions.
  </proof>

  <subsection*|Other outcome spaces and joint observables><label|sec:structured>

  The representation\UPVM theorem applies without change to every
  second-countable locally compact Hausdorff space: the proof uses
  Riesz\UMarkov, a compact exhaustion and continuous functions generating the
  Borel sets, not smooth coordinates. The multiplication model on
  <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|M,\<nu\>|)>> is immediate: multiplication by
  <math|1<rsub|B>> gives its projections, and
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|u,E<around|(|B|)>*u|\<rangle\>>=<big|int><rsub|B><around|\||u|\|><rsup|2>*d*\<nu\>>.
  For a Hermitian vector bundle use the fibre inner product in the same
  formula; multiplication by a scalar function still has norm at most its
  supremum norm.

  For a countable discrete space, <math|P<rsub|x>=E<around|(|<around|{|x|}>|)>>
  are orthogonal and sum strongly to <math|I>. Conversely such a family
  defines <math|E<around|(|B|)>=<big|sum><rsub|x\<in\>B>P<rsub|x>>:
  orthogonality gives norm convergence on each vector since
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|x><around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|x>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>.
  The same identity proves strong convergence of
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|x>f<around|(|x|)>*P<rsub|x>> for bounded <math|f>,
  multiplicativity, and the asserted bounded calculus. On an arbitrary
  measurable outcome space, a normalized PVM gives a unital bounded Borel
  calculus by simple approximation. Conversely a unital homomorphism with
  bounded pointwise weak continuity gives
  <math|E<around|(|B|)>=j<around|(|1<rsub|B>|)>>; orthogonality and
  continuity of indicators of increasing unions give a PVM, as in
  <notes-link|the Part I construction|self-adjoint-operators-proofs.tm#sec:bounded>.

  Here is the local-compactness point for an infinite-dimensional Hilbert
  space <math|X>. If a continuous function <math|f> vanishing at infinity
  were nonzero at <math|x>, the compact set
  <math|<around|{|<around|\||f|\|>\<geq\><around|\||f<around|(|x|)>|\|>/2|}>>
  would contain an open neighbourhood of <math|x>. It would therefore contain
  a closed ball of positive radius, which would be compact. But if
  <math|<around|(|e<rsub|n>|)>> is an orthonormal sequence, the points
  <math|x+r*e<rsub|n>> of such a ball have mutual distance <math|r*<sqrt|2>>,
  so admit no convergent subsequence. This contradiction proves
  <math|C<rsub|0><around|(|X|)>=0>. No analogous argument prevents normalized
  PVMs on its Borel sets.

  <\proposition>
    [Joint sharp observables and operations on
    outcomes]<label|prop:joint-outcomes> Let <math|E<rsub|1>,E<rsub|2>> be
    normalized PVMs on second-countable locally compact Hausdorff spaces
    <math|X,Y>, acting on the same Hilbert space. They are marginals of a
    joint PVM on <math|X\<times\>Y> if and only if
    <math|E<rsub|1><around|(|B|)>*E<rsub|2><around|(|C|)>=E<rsub|2><around|(|C|)>*E<rsub|1><around|(|B|)>>
    for all Borel sets <math|B,C>. The joint PVM is unique and satisfies
    <math|E*<around|(|B\<times\>C|)>=E<rsub|1><around|(|B|)>*E<rsub|2><around|(|C|)>>.
    A Borel map from <math|X\<times\>Y> to another such space can then be
    applied by pushforward. This includes group multiplication when
    <math|X=Y=G> is a second-countable locally compact group.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    For a joint PVM, multiply the projections of the cylinder sets
    <math|B\<times\>Y> and <math|X\<times\>C>. Their intersection is
    <math|B\<times\>C>, which proves necessity and the rectangle formula.
    Scalar measure uniqueness on the sigma-algebra generated by rectangles
    proves uniqueness.

    For sufficiency the two bounded calculi commute, first for simple
    functions and then by strong limits. Let <math|\<cal-A\>> be the
    commutative unital <math|C<rsup|\<ast\>>>-algebra generated by <math|I>
    and their <math|C<rsub|0>> calculi in <math|B<around|(|H|)>>. It is
    <math|C<around|(|K|)>> for its compact character space <math|K>, with a
    spectral measure <math|P> on <math|K>. A character restricted to either
    <math|C<rsub|0>> calculus is either zero or evaluation at a point of the
    corresponding outcome space: this follows from the locally compact
    Gelfand theorem, even if that calculus is not faithful. Write the zero
    restriction as the extra point <math|\<infty\>> of <math|X<rsup|+>> or
    <math|Y<rsup|+>>; for a compact space use an extra isolated point. The
    resulting maps <math|a:K\<to\>X<rsup|+>> and <math|b:K\<to\>Y<rsup|+>>
    are continuous, since all their pulled-back continuous functions are
    Gelfand transforms of the generating elements.

    Push <math|P> forward under <math|<around|(|a,b|)>>. Its marginal on
    <math|X<rsup|+>> integrates every <math|f\<in\>C<rsub|0><around|(|X|)>>
    to the first calculus. If <math|\<chi\><rsub|n>*\<uparrow\>*1> are
    continuous compactly supported cutoffs on <math|X>, nondegeneracy and
    bounded convergence give <math|P<around|(|a<rsup|-1><around|(|X|)>|)>=lim<rsub|n>
    j<rsub|1><around|(|\<chi\><rsub|n>|)>=I> strongly. Thus the marginal has
    zero mass at <math|\<infty\>>; similarly for <math|Y>. The measure is
    concentrated on <math|X\<times\>Y> and has the required marginals by
    uniqueness of the scalar representing measures. This constructs the joint
    PVM. The already proved substitution rule gives the pushforward
    statement, with no properness assumption on the Borel map.
  </proof>

  <section|Observables, normal states and probability
  laws><label|sec:probability>

  This section supplies the operator-algebra argument for Section 4 of
  <notes-link|Part II|self-adjoint-operators-extensions.tm#sec:probability>.
  Here the von Neumann algebra contains the bounded tests of each observable,
  while the normal state turns its spectral projections into probabilities.

  <\proposition>
    [Affiliated self-adjoint observables and laws]<label|prop:affiliated> For
    <math|A=A<rsup|\<ast\>>> and a von Neumann algebra
    <math|\<cal-M\>\<subset\>B<around|(|H|)>>, the following are equivalent:
    <math|A> commutes, with domain invariance, with all unitaries of
    <math|\<cal-M\><rprime|'>>; its spectral projections lie in
    <math|\<cal-M\>>; its bounded Borel calculus lies in <math|\<cal-M\>>;
    its resolvents lie in <math|\<cal-M\>>. In a normal state
    <math|\<omega\>>, <math|\<omega\><around|(|E<rsub|A><around|(|\<cdot\>|)>|)>>
    is a probability measure and <math|\<omega\><around|(|f<around|(|A|)>|)>=<big|int>f*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Domain commutation passes to bounded inverses
    <math|R<rsub|A><around|(|z|)>>, and the bicommutant theorem places these
    inverses in <math|\<cal-M\>>. Resolvent generation and strong closure
    place the <math|C<rsub|0>> calculus and then the Borel calculus in
    <math|\<cal-M\>>. Conversely spectral projections in <math|\<cal-M\>>
    commute with <math|\<cal-M\><rprime|'>>; their truncations show that
    commuting unitaries preserve the spectral domain and commute with
    <math|A>. The projections and bounded calculus implications are immediate
    from integration. Normality of the state applied to increasing sums of
    disjoint projections gives countable additivity and total mass one. The
    integral identity holds first for simple functions and then by bounded
    convergence. For a single observable the law is classical; a joint
    projection-valued measure for two observables would make their marginal
    projections commute. Noncommuting marginal projections therefore preclude
    such a joint measure.
  </proof>

  <subsection*|Ordered tests are not determined by marginal laws>

  <\proposition>
    [A matrix example of ordered expectations]<label|prop:ordered-tests>
    Let

    <\equation*>
      A=\<sigma\><rsub|1>=<matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|1>>|<row|<cell|1>|<cell|0>>>>>,
      <space|2em>B=\<sigma\><rsub|2>=<matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|-i>>|<row|<cell|i>|<cell|0>>>>>,
      <space|2em>u=<binom|1|0>.
    </equation*>

    In the state <math|\<omega\><around*|(|T|)>=<around*|\<langle\>|u,T u|\<rangle\>>>, both spectral laws equal
    <math|<around*|(|\<delta\><rsub|-1>+\<delta\><rsub|1>|)>/2>, whereas
    <math|\<omega\><around*|(|A*B|)>=i> and <math|\<omega\><around*|(|B*A|)>=-i>. The pair
    <math|<around*|(|A,-B|)>> has the same two marginal laws and a different mixed
    expectation.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Direct multiplication gives <math|A<rsup|2>=B<rsup|2>=I> and
    <math|A=A<rsup|\<ast\>>,B=B<rsup|\<ast\>>>. Thus their spectral
    projections for the values <math|\<pm\>1> are
    <math|<around*|(|I\<pm\>A|)>/2> and <math|<around*|(|I\<pm\>B|)>/2>. The diagonal matrix
    entries <math|<around*|\<langle\>|u,A u|\<rangle\>>> and
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|u,B u|\<rangle\>>> are zero, so each projection has
    probability one half. Also <math|A*B=i*<math-up|diag><around*|(|1,-1|)>> and
    <math|B*A=-i*<math-up|diag><around*|(|1,-1|)>>, giving the asserted expectations. Negating
    <math|B> interchanges its two equally weighted spectral projections and
    changes the sign of <math|A*B>. This proves that the marginal laws do not
    determine the ordered expectation.

    For any state, positivity implies
    <math|\<omega\><around*|(|T<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>=<wide|\<omega\><around*|(|T|)>|\<bar\>>>.
    For completeness, the expectation of a bounded self-adjoint operator is
    real, since after adding a sufficiently large multiple of <math|I> it
    becomes positive. Decompose an arbitrary <math|T> into its real and
    imaginary self-adjoint parts to obtain the identity. Thus for real
    bounded tests <math|f,g>, the expectations of <math|j<rsub|1><around*|(|f|)>j<rsub|2><around*|(|g|)>>
    and <math|j<rsub|2><around*|(|g|)>j<rsub|1><around*|(|f|)>> are complex conjugates. All finite
    ordered words are well-defined bounded operators, since
    <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|j<rsub|k<rsub|1>><around*|(|f<rsub|1>|)>\<cdots\>j<rsub|k<rsub|m>><around*|(|f<rsub|m>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|r=1><rsup|m><around*|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|r>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>.
    Their state expectations constitute the joint bounded-test distribution.
  </proof>

  <subsection*|Continuous-test laws and spectral-event probabilities>

  Let <math|j:C<rsub|0><around*|(|\<bbb-R\>|)>\<rightarrow\>\<cal-M\>> be a
  nondegenerate representation and <math|\<omega\>> any state. The positive
  functional <math|\<ell\>=\<omega\>\<circ\>j> has norm at most one.
  Riesz\UMarkov gives a finite positive measure <math|\<mu\>> with
  <math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<bbb-R\>|)>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<ell\>|\<\|\|\>>>. For
  increasing compactly supported cutoffs <math|\<chi\><rsub|n>\<uparrow\>1>,
  monotone convergence of this measure gives

  <\equation*>
    \<mu\><around*|(|\<bbb-R\>|)>=lim<rsub|n>\<omega\><around*|(|j<around*|(|\<chi\><rsub|n>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Hence full mass is exactly the cutoff condition stated in the post. If
  the state is normal, <math|j<around*|(|\<chi\><rsub|n>|)>\<uparrow\>I> strongly and
  normality makes the limit one. The preceding proposition then identifies
  this measure with <math|B\<mapsto\>\<omega\><around*|(|E<around*|(|B|)>|)>>. The following
  example shows why full mass alone does not give that identification.

  <\proposition>
    [Full mass does not imply spectral normality]<label|prop:nonnormal-law>
    On <math|H=L<rsup|2><around*|(|0,1|)>> let <math|A> multiply by <math|t>. There is a
    state <math|\<omega\>> on <math|B<around*|(|H|)>> such that
    <math|\<omega\><around*|(|f<around*|(|A|)>|)>=f<around*|(|0|)>> for every bounded continuous real-line test
    <math|f>, although <math|\<omega\><around*|(|E<rsub|A><around*|(|<around*|{|0|}>|)>|)>=0>. Its restriction
    to <math|C<rsub|0><around*|(|\<bbb-R\>|)>> has norm one and Riesz law
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>>, but the state is not normal.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Put <math|u<rsub|n>=<sqrt|n>*1<rsub|<around*|(|0,1/n|)>>>. These are unit vectors.
    Fix a free ultrafilter <math|\<cal-U\>> on the positive integers, and
    define

    <\equation*>
      \<omega\><around*|(|T|)>=lim<rsub|n\<rightarrow\>\<cal-U\>>
      <around*|\<langle\>|u<rsub|n>,T u<rsub|n>|\<rangle\>>.
    </equation*>

    Each bounded complex sequence has a unique ultrafilter limit in a
    compact disc; such limits preserve addition, scalar multiplication and
    nonnegativity, as follows by intersecting the sets on which each
    sequence lies near its limit. Thus <math|\<omega\>> is linear and
    positive, <math|\<omega\><around*|(|I|)>=1>, and
    <math|<around*|\||\<omega\><around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|T|\<\|\|\>>>.
    It is a state. If <math|f> is bounded and continuous, then

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|u<rsub|n>,f<around*|(|A|)>u<rsub|n>|\<rangle\>>
      =n<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1/n>f<around*|(|t|)>\<mathd\>t\<rightarrow\>f<around*|(|0|)>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed the difference is bounded by
    <math|sup<rsub|0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1/n><around*|\||f<around*|(|t|)>-f<around*|(|0|)>|\|>>.
    The ultrafilter limit agrees with the ordinary limit. A continuous
    compactly supported cutoff equal to one on <math|<around*|[|0,1|]>> satisfies
    <math|f<around*|(|A|)>=I>; thus the restriction has norm one. Uniqueness in
    Riesz\UMarkov identifies its law as <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>>.

    The spectral projections of <math|A> multiply by indicators of Borel
    subsets of <math|<around*|(|0,1|)>>, so <math|E<rsub|A><around*|(|<around*|{|0|}>|)>=0>. To see failure of
    normality directly, let <math|P<rsub|k>=E<rsub|A><around*|(|<around*|(|0,1/k|)>|)>>. These
    projections decrease strongly to zero by dominated convergence, but
    <math|\<omega\><around*|(|P<rsub|k>|)>=1> for every <math|k>, since
    <math|P<rsub|k>u<rsub|n>=u<rsub|n>> whenever <math|n\<geqslant\>k>.
    Equivalently <math|I-P<rsub|k>\<uparrow\>I> while all their
    expectations are zero. This contradicts normality.
  </proof>

  <section|The geometric commutator and the distance
  formula><label|sec:geometry>

  <\proposition>
    [Compact resolvent and vanishing-at-infinity functions] For self-adjoint
    <math|D>, compactness of one nonreal resolvent is equivalent to
    compactness of every <math|f<around|(|D|)>>,
    <math|f\<in\>C<rsub|0><around|(|\<bbb-R\>|)>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    One compact resolvent and its adjoint generate only compact operators
    under sums, products and norm limits without a constant term. Resolvent
    density therefore gives all <math|C<rsub|0>> multipliers. The converse
    follows by choosing <math|f<around|(|x|)>=<around|(|x-i|)><rsup|-1>>.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    [The commutative spectral distance] On a compact connected Riemannian
    spin manifold without boundary, let <math|D> be its Dirac operator on
    square-integrable spinors. For real smooth <math|f>,
    <math|<around|[|D,f|]>> is bounded with norm <math|sup
    <around|\||d*f|\|>>, and

    <\equation*>
      d*<around|(|p,q|)>=sup <around|{|<around|\||f<around|(|p|)>-f<around|(|q|)>|\|>:f\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>>*<around|(|M,\<bbb-R\>|)>,<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|[|D,f|]>|\<\|\|\>>\<le\>1|}>.
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    The geometric input is the local expression
    <math|D=-i*<big|sum><rsub|j>c<around|(|e<rsup|j>|)>*\<nabla\><rsub|e<rsub|j>>>
    for an orthonormal coframe, with Clifford multiplication satisfying
    <math|c<around|(|\<xi\>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*c<around|(|\<xi\>|)>=<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|2>*I>
    for real covectors (the factor <math|i> moves with conventions). The
    Leibniz rule gives <math|<around|[|D,f|]>=-i*c*<around|(|d*f|)>>. Its
    pointwise operator norm is <math|<around|\||d*f|\|>>; the norm of this
    multiplication operator on <math|L<rsup|2>> is its essential supremum,
    equal to its supremum by continuity. Multiplication by smooth <math|f>
    preserves the <math|H<rsup|1>> domain.

    For clarity, that is indeed the self-adjoint domain. Integration by parts
    on a boundaryless manifold makes <math|D> symmetric. The elliptic energy
    estimate <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|H<rsup|1>>\<le\>C*<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|D*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>+<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>|)>>
    follows by integrating the identity <math|D<rsup|2>=\<nabla\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<nabla\>+<math-up|Scal>/4>
    and using bounded curvature on the compact manifold. If
    <math|u\<in\>D<around|(|D<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>>, then distributionally
    <math|D*u\<in\>L<rsup|2>>. Local smoothing and a partition of unity give
    smooth <math|u<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<to\>u> with
    <math|D*u<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> bounded in <math|L<rsup|2>>: the
    commutator of a smooth-coefficient first-order operator with convolution
    is uniformly <math|L<rsup|2>> bounded, as its kernel contains
    <math|<around|(|a<around|(|x|)>-a<around|(|y|)>|)>*\<partial\>*\<eta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*<around|(|x-y|)>>
    and bounded coefficient-derivative terms, whose integral bounds are
    uniform by <math|<around|\||a<around|(|x|)>-a<around|(|y|)>|\|>\<le\>C*<around|\||x-y|\|>>.
    The energy estimate bounds <math|u<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> in
    <math|H<rsup|1>>; weak compactness gives <math|u\<in\>H<rsup|1>>.
    Conversely every <math|H<rsup|1>> spinor is in the adjoint domain by
    smooth approximation. Thus <math|D=D<rsup|\<ast\>>> on <math|H<rsup|1>>.
    The compact embedding <math|H<rsup|1>\<hookrightarrow\>L<rsup|2>> makes
    its resolvent compact.

    If <math|sup <around|\||d*f|\|>\<le\>1>, integration along any piecewise
    smooth path from <math|p> to <math|q> gives
    <math|<around|\||f<around|(|p|)>-f<around|(|q|)>|\|>\<le\>> its length.
    Infimizing proves one inequality. For the reverse, let
    <math|g<around|(|x|)>=d*<around|(|x,q|)>>, a one-Lipschitz function. We
    detail the smoothing step. Cover <math|M> by finitely many sufficiently
    small coordinate balls on which metric norms differ from fixed Euclidean
    norms by a factor at most <math|1+\<eta\>>, and choose a subordinate
    smooth partition <math|\<chi\><rsub|j>>. Mollify <math|g> on slightly
    larger balls, obtaining <math|g<rsub|j>> uniformly within
    <math|\<delta\>> of <math|g> and with
    <math|<around|\||d*g<rsub|j>|\|>\<le\><around|(|1+\<eta\>|)><rsup|2>>.
    For <math|g<rsub|\<delta\>>=<big|sum>\<chi\><rsub|j>*g<rsub|j>>,

    <\equation*>
      d*g<rsub|\<delta\>>=<big|sum>\<chi\><rsub|j>*d*g<rsub|j>+<big|sum><around|(|g<rsub|j>-g|)>*d*\<chi\><rsub|j>,
    </equation*>

    because <math|<big|sum>d*\<chi\><rsub|j>=0>. Thus
    <math|<around|\||d*g<rsub|\<delta\>>|\|>\<le\><around|(|1+\<eta\>|)><rsup|2>+\<delta\>*<big|sum><around|\<\|\|\>|d*\<chi\><rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>.
    Choose first <math|\<eta\>>, then <math|\<delta\>>, to make this at most
    <math|1+\<varepsilon\>>, while <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|g<rsub|\<delta\>>-g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<to\>0>.
    The admissible functions <math|g<rsub|\<delta\>>/<around|(|1+\<varepsilon\>|)>>
    have differences tending to <math|d*<around|(|p,q|)>>, proving equality.
    This proof also identifies the geometric information absent from the
    calculus of <math|D> alone: its commutators use the separately specified
    multiplication algebra.
  </proof>

  <section|Sharp observables and Poisson brackets><label|sec:quantization>

  This section justifies the obstruction in the final section of the post.
  Let <math|\<cal-A\>\<subseteq\>C<rsub|0><around*|(|M|)>> be a complex algebra of
  smooth tests, stable under conjugation and the Poisson bracket. For
  <math|\<hbar\>\<gtr\>0> tending to zero, consider linear maps
  <math|Q<rsub|\<hbar\>>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\>B<around*|(|H<rsub|\<hbar\>>|)>> that
  respect adjoints. The operator norms of the multiplication and Dirac
  defects in the post are well-defined, even if the Hilbert spaces vary.
  The norm correspondence
  <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsub|\<hbar\>><around*|(|f|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<rightarrow\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>
  prevents the classical limit from losing a nonzero test. These conditions
  describe requirements on a proposed family, not an assertion that one
  exists on every symplectic manifold.

  <\proposition>
    [An exact sharp calculus cannot quantize a nonzero bracket]
    Suppose all the <math|Q<rsub|\<hbar\>>> are
    <math|\<ast\>>-homomorphisms, and assume both norm correspondence for
    every test and the Dirac condition for every pair. Then
    <math|<around*|{|f,g|}>=0> for all <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Pointwise products in <math|\<cal-A\>> commute, so
    <math|<around*|[|Q<rsub|\<hbar\>><around*|(|f|)>,Q<rsub|\<hbar\>><around*|(|g|)>|]>=Q<rsub|\<hbar\>><around*|(|f*g-g*f|)>=0>.
    The Dirac condition therefore says
    <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsub|\<hbar\>><around*|(|<around*|{|f,g|}>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<rightarrow\>0>.
    Since <math|<around*|{|f,g|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>, norm correspondence gives the same
    limit as <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|{|f,g|}>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>.
    Hence the bracket is zero. In fact this proof requires only that the
    image of each <math|Q<rsub|\<hbar\>>> commute, not its full
    multiplicativity.
  </proof>

  The obstruction applies to the full compactly supported smooth test
  algebra on a positive-dimensional symplectic manifold. In a Darboux chart
  centred at <math|p>, choose canonical coordinates <math|q<rsub|1>,p<rsub|1>>
  with <math|<around*|{|q<rsub|1>,p<rsub|1>|}>=1>. Multiply each by a smooth cutoff
  compactly supported in the chart and equal to one near <math|p>, then
  extend them by zero. The resulting <math|f,g\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|M|)>>
  have <math|<around*|{|f,g|}><around*|(|p|)>=1>, because the cutoff derivatives vanish there.
  Thus their bracket is nonzero, and the proposition rules out a family of
  sharp calculi with those classical-limit properties.

  Positivity of a quantization map is a different condition: it says
  <math|Q<rsub|\<hbar\>><around*|(|f|)>\<geqslant\>0> for nonnegative tests, and does
  not force commuting images. Actual constructions, and the continuous-field
  structure used in strict quantization, require further work; see the
  Landsman references below. No additional existence theorem is used here.

  <section|References and further reading><label|sec:references>

  <\itemize>
    <item>M. Reed and B. Simon, <with|font-shape|italic|Methods of Modern
    Mathematical Physics, I: Functional Analysis>, revised edition, Academic
    Press, 1980; and <with|font-shape|italic|II: Fourier Analysis,
    Self-Adjointness>, 1975. Volume I develops spectral theory and
    convergence; Volume II, Theorems X.37\UX.38, treats Nelson and
    Faris\ULavine. <hlink|Volume I|https://www.sciencedirect.com/book/9780125850018/methods-of-modern-mathematical-physics>;
    <hlink|Volume II bibliographic record|https://scholars.duke.edu/publication/1163833>.

    <item>G. Teschl, <with|font-shape|italic|Mathematical Methods in Quantum
    Mechanics>, second edition, AMS, 2014. A complementary textbook treatment
    of domains, spectral measures, extensions and perturbations; Section 4.2
    develops the commutation criteria used above. <hlink|Author's text and
    book page|https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/>.

    <item>E. Nelson, <em|Analytic vectors>, Annals of Mathematics (2)
    <with|font-series|bold|70> (1959), 572\U615.
    <hlink|doi:10.2307/1970331|https://doi.org/10.2307/1970331>.
    Analytic vectors, integration of infinitesimal representations and the
    distinction between algebraic and strong commutation.

    <item>J. van Neerven, <em|Functional Analysis>, Cambridge University
    Press, 2022. Chapters 9\U10 treat bounded and unbounded normal operators;
    Chapter 15 connects states and observables. <hlink|Chapter
    10|https://doi.org/10.1017/9781009232487.012>.

    <item>G. W. Mackey, <em|Imprimitivity for Representations of Locally
    Compact Groups I>, Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences
    <with|font-series|bold|35> (1949), 537\U545.
    <hlink|doi:10.1073/pnas.35.9.537|https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.35.9.537>.
    Covariant spectral measures on homogeneous outcome spaces and their
    relation to induced representations.

    <item>N. P. Landsman, <em|Strict quantization of coadjoint orbits>
    (1998), <hlink|arXiv:math-ph/9807027|https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/9807027>.
    Positive quantization maps for integral coadjoint orbits of compact
    connected Lie groups. See also <em|Foundations of Quantum Theory: From
    Classical Concepts to Operator Algebras>, Springer, 2017,
    <hlink|open-access book|https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-51777-3>, for
    the relation between classical structures, states and quantum operator
    algebras.

    <item>A. Connes, <with|font-shape|italic|Noncommutative Geometry>,
    Academic Press, 1994, <hlink|author's
    book|https://alainconnes.org/wp-content/uploads/book94bigpdf.pdf>; and
    \PNoncommutative geometry and reality\Q, <with|font-shape|italic|Journal
    of Mathematical Physics> 36 (1995), 6194\U6231, <hlink|author's
    text|https://alainconnes.org/wp-content/uploads/reality.pdf>. For the
    algebra\Uspace correspondence and the role of the Dirac commutator in
    metric geometry.

    <item>I. E. Segal, <em|Postulates for General Quantum Mechanics>, Annals
    of Mathematics (2) <with|font-series|bold|48> (1947), 930\U948.
    <hlink|Original paper|https://www.jstor.org/stable/1969387>. An early
    algebraic formulation of quantum mechanics.
  </itemize>

  Return to <notes-link|Observables II: Beyond the real line|self-adjoint-operators-extensions.tm>.

  <with|font-series|bold|Earlier proof companions.> <notes-link|I. The basic idea|self-adjoint-operators-proofs.tm>.
